\section{The geometry}{

\code{ANGLEL} and \code{ANGLER} are, per handbook 8.A.2, "the declination angles, in
degrees, below the horizon of a sighting (to the left or right, respectively)
\strong{when it is perpendicular to the track-line}". Because the angle is taken at
the moment the sighting is abeam, the aircraft, the point on the sea surface
directly below it, and the animal form a right triangle in the vertical plane
perpendicular to the track. The perpendicular distance is therefore

\deqn{x = h / \tan(\theta)}

for altitude \eqn{h} and declination \eqn{\theta}. An angle of 90 degrees is
straight down and gives a distance of zero; as the angle approaches the
horizon the distance grows without bound.

These replaced \code{STRIP} in 2022. Handbook 8.A.2 gives the reason: survey
altitudes had to rise once offshore wind turbines were in place, and the fixed
distance intervals \code{STRIP} encodes shift with altitude, whereas an angle
carries the altitude dependence explicitly.
}

\section{Units}{

\code{ALT} is in metres (8.A.1), so distances are returned in metres by default.
Note that segment effort in this package is in \strong{kilometres} — if you pass
both to \code{Distance::ds()} you must either use \code{units = "km"} here or supply
\code{convert_units}. Getting this wrong scales density estimates by 1000.
}

